Y1  - 2026//
N2  - This study examines the long-term effects of warfare trauma on posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) symptoms in older veterans, focusing on trauma reminde [...]

Impact Statement
Fifty years after the Yom Kippur War, many veterans continue to experience psychological distress, with symptoms exacerbated by contemporary trauma reminders. This study highlights the role of meaning in life as a protective factor that moderates the impact of war-related trauma, even decades later. Policymakers should prioritize long-term mental health interventions that not only address posttraumatic stress disorder symptoms but also foster meaning-making processes, resilience, and social integration among aging veterans, particularly during periods of national crisis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
